@node AI assistant
@chapter AI assistant

@cindex AI
@cindex assistant
@cindex Ollama
@cindex Claude
@cindex agent
@cindex chat
@cindex large language model

Where a language server (@pxref{Language servers}) @emph{understands} your code,
the @dfn{AI assistant} lets you @emph{talk} to a large language model from
inside the editor: ask a question, have it rewrite the open file, plan a change
across several files, or run as an autonomous agent -- all in the bottom pane,
next to compiler and debugger output, driven from the keyboard like everything
else.

The assistant is @strong{off unless you ask for it}, twice over.  It is compiled
only when xwpe is configured with @option{--enable-ai}, so a normal build
contains none of it and is byte-for-byte the classic editor.  Even in a build
that has it, it does nothing until you turn it on.  This is deliberate: the
appeal is @dfn{the best old interface, optionally superpowered}, never
@dfn{another AI editor that happens to look retro}.

It is also @strong{local-first}.  The default backend is Ollama
(@uref{https://ollama.com}) running on your own machine -- no account, no API
key, no telemetry, and it works over SSH.  Cloud models are opt-in.

@section Enabling the assistant

@cindex AI, enabling
Configure and build with the feature on:

@example
./configure --enable-ai                    # local backends only
./configure --enable-ai --enable-ai-tls    # also HTTPS (Claude, remote OpenAI)
make
@end example

Then turn it on at run time in one of two ways: tick @samp{Ai assistant} under
@samp{Options}, @samp{Editor}, or start with @env{XWPE_AI_ENABLE=1}.  With it on
a clickable @samp{Alt-B AI} entry appears on the bottom status bar and
@key{Alt-B} opens the assistant's action menu -- the same way @key{Alt-F} opens
the @samp{File} menu.  Pick an entry with the arrow keys and @key{Enter}, or
press its letter; the letter also works as type-ahead, so @kbd{Alt-B a} jumps
straight to Ask (programming mode only, i.e.@: run as @command{wpe} or
@command{xwpe}):

@table @kbd
@item Alt-B a
Ask -- chat.
@item Alt-B e
Edit the current file.
@item Alt-B p
Plan a multi-file change.
@item Alt-B g
Agent -- an autonomous loop.
@item Alt-B m
pick the Model.
@item Alt-B y
cycle the permission policy (ask / edits / auto).
@item Alt-B n
start a New session.
@end table

The same actions are on the @samp{AI} menu, which also holds the enable toggle,
the policy radio, the model picker and @samp{New Session}.

@section Backends

@cindex AI, backends
The backend is chosen with @code{AIBackend} (or @env{XWPE_AI_BACKEND}); the same
chat/edit/plan/agent interface works with all of them.

@table @asis
@item @code{ollama} (default)
Ollama's HTTP API on @code{http://localhost:11434}.  Local, free, no key.  The
model list comes from the server, and @kbd{Alt-B m} picks one.
@item @code{openai}
Any OpenAI-compatible server -- @command{llama.cpp}, LM Studio, vLLM, or OpenAI
itself.  Set @code{AIEndpoint} to its URL; an API key comes from
@env{OPENAI_API_KEY}.
@item @code{claude}
Anthropic's Messages API.  Needs @option{--enable-ai-tls} and
@env{ANTHROPIC_API_KEY}.
@item @code{claudecli}
Runs the @command{claude} command-line tool (Claude Code) as a subprocess, using
@emph{its} login.  No API key, no extra dependency, and it draws on your Claude
subscription rather than per-token API billing.  Offered only when
@command{claude} is on @env{PATH}.
@item @code{mock}
A deterministic in-process backend used by the test suite; no network.
@end table

@section Chat

@cindex AI, chat
@kbd{Alt-B a} asks for a prompt and streams the reply into the @samp{AI} pane.
The current file (and the selection, if any) is sent as context, so ``what does
this function do?'' works.  The editor stays fully interactive while the reply
streams; @key{Esc} cancels.  The conversation is remembered for the workspace
(see @emph{Sessions}, below).

@section Edit

@cindex AI, edit
@kbd{Alt-B e} asks for an instruction (``add error handling'', ``fix the compile
error''), sends the current file, and shows the model's rewrite as a @dfn{diff}
you review hunk by hunk: @kbd{y} applies this hunk, @kbd{n} skips it, @kbd{a}
applies all the rest, @kbd{q} cancels.  Applying uses one whole-buffer undo
step, so @kbd{Ctrl-U} reverts the whole edit.  Nothing is written to disk until
you @emph{save}.

@section Plan (multi-file)

@cindex AI, plan
@kbd{Alt-B p} tackles a change that spans several files.  The assistant first
@dfn{studies} the workspace -- the files you have open, plus the members of the
open project (@pxref{Projects}) and, if asked, the surrounding folder -- reading
whatever it needs with read-only tools.  It then proposes a @dfn{plan}: the
complete new content for each file it wants to change.

xwpe collects the proposal without touching anything and shows the list with
per-file line counts:

@example
The AI proposes to change 3 files:
   src/parser.c  (+12 -4)
   src/parser.h  (+2  -0)
   src/main.c    (+1  -1)
 a = apply all    f = review file by file    q = cancel
@end example

@kbd{a} applies every file; @kbd{f} opens each and lets you accept it hunk by
hunk.  Each changed file is @strong{opened in its own window and left open}, so
you can read it, step between them with @kbd{Alt-1}@dots{}, and undo any one
with @kbd{Ctrl-U}.

@section Agent and the permission dial

@cindex AI, agent
@cindex AI, permissions
@kbd{Alt-B g} runs the model as an autonomous agent with a small, bounded set of
tools: @code{read_file}, @code{list_dir} and @code{grep} run on their own, while
@code{write_file} and @code{run_command} are governed by the @dfn{permission
dial}:

@table @asis
@item @code{ask} (default)
every write or command asks you @kbd{y}/@kbd{n}.
@item @code{edits}
writes go through automatically; commands still ask.
@item @code{auto}
everything runs unattended; you review the result afterwards.
@end table

Set the dial from the @samp{AI} menu, with @kbd{Alt-B y}, or with @code{AIPolicy}
/ @env{XWPE_AI_POLICY}.  @code{edits} and @code{auto} always take a checkpoint
first.  For the @code{claudecli} backend the dial maps onto the CLI's own
@option{--permission-mode} so the same rules apply.

@section Reviewing changes like compile errors

@cindex AI, review
@cindex AI, checkpoint
@cindex AI, revert
Before an @code{edits} or @code{auto} run, xwpe takes a @dfn{checkpoint}: a
@command{git} reference when the workspace is a git tree, otherwise a private
snapshot of the files under @file{~/.xwpe/checkpoints/}.  Afterwards it writes
the @dfn{changeset} into the @samp{Messages} window, one line per changed hunk in
the compiler's @code{file:line:} format -- so you step through the AI's changes
with the very same @kbd{Alt-T} (Next) and @kbd{Alt-V} (Previous) keys you use for
compile errors, each jump opening the file at the change.

A review prompt then offers @kbd{a} keep all, @kbd{r} revert everything back to
the checkpoint, @kbd{f} decide file by file, and (in a git tree) @kbd{c} commit
the change as one auditable commit.  Reverting a file the agent created deletes
it.  This makes trying a weaker local model in @code{auto} safe: if it makes a
mess, one @kbd{r} puts you back.

@section Sessions

@cindex AI, sessions
The conversation is tied to the workspace and resumes automatically.  For
@code{claudecli} xwpe keeps the CLI's own session id and passes @option{--resume};
for the other backends it keeps a short transcript that seeds the next prompt.
It is stored beside the project file (as @file{.xwpe-ai-session}) when a project
is open, otherwise under @file{~/.xwpe/ai/}.  @kbd{Alt-B n} forgets the current
session and starts fresh.

@section Configuration

@cindex AI, configuration
These keys live in the @code{[Programming]} section of @file{xwperc}
(@pxref{Configuration}) and are written by @samp{Save Options}:

@table @code
@item AIBackend
@code{ollama} (0), @code{openai} (1), @code{claude} (2), @code{claudecli} (4).
@item AIEndpoint
Base URL for the HTTP backends (default @code{http://localhost:11434}).
@item AIModel
Model name; empty means ``pick the first the server offers''.
@item AIPolicy
@code{ask}, @code{edits} or @code{auto}.
@end table

Environment overrides (handy for scripts and tests): @env{XWPE_AI_ENABLE},
@env{XWPE_AI_BACKEND}, @env{XWPE_AI_ENDPOINT}, @env{XWPE_AI_MODEL},
@env{XWPE_AI_POLICY}, and @env{XWPE_AI_TRACE} (a path to log assistant events
to).  API keys are read from the standard environment variable first
(@env{ANTHROPIC_API_KEY}, @env{OPENAI_API_KEY}), then from @code{@var{NAME}_FILE},
then from @file{~/.config/xwpe/anthropic-api-key} (or @file{openai-api-key}); the
@code{claudecli} backend needs none of this.
